refusalEndsT -- SEQ 535 R1, SEQ 537: after a refusal the new road ends the action, as the old road does; the unit is the
action, not its caller. x = hz - 1 refuses (hz is a holder, SEQ 504 R0). Every statement after it, every further pass of a
loop, and either arm of an if on a refused condition must not run. Each new-road row has its old-road twin, the oracle. The
caller rows are the non-zero sibling: the caller runs on and writes 7, because the refusal ended the callee only.
A standing refusal silences operators, so most shapes wind down by themselves; while1, do1 and ifcall are the shapes that
go wrong without opWhile's, opDo's and opIf's guards: a literal condition never ends, and an action called in the else arm
clears the refusal on its way out, so the action runs on (tail 1). oWhile1 and oDo1 are defined and NOT called: the OLD road
HANGS on both, measured 2026-11-06 (exit 142 under an alarm), so they have no twin row here. Without opFor's guard the body runs again and the refusal prints once per pass, which the refusal count row reads.
